ignificant discrepancy—the timestamp suggests April 14 at 15:00 CST, but the record name says \"14:11\", which would imply this task ran on April 17 based on the context. There's either a timezone mismatch or the timestamp conversion is off by a day. The memory entries are consistently showing timestamps that are roughly 2-4 hours in the future relative to the record names—like recvh3nAPnjZ1K says 13:11 but actually contains 09:00 UTC, which is 17:00 CST. The discrepancy suggests the timestamps are being recorded in UTC while the names reflect a CST time that's about 4 hours ahead. I'm realizing the timezone conversion is the culprit—the cron fires at 14:11 CST, but the heartbeat timestamp is being recorded in UTC as 06:11, which is 8 hours behind. The record names showing the CST time while the actual timestamp reflects UTC are creating this confusing mismatch between what the name suggests and what the data actually shows.\n\nThe real issue is that the 14:11 check record appears to have a status field showing \"进行中\" (in progress) when it should probably be marked as complete, and I'm seeing records from 16:11 that I can't locate in the current list—suggesting there might be pagination or data retrieval issues with the API call. I'm noticing a discrepancy—the 16:11 record from April 17th is showing a timestamp that converts to 2026-04-14, which doesn't match. The record name says \"14:11\" but the actual creation time seems to be from April 17th, suggesting there's a timezone or timestamp conversion issue happening with these records. The timestamp conversion is definitely off—1744892400000 ms should be April 14 at 15:00 CST, not April 17. But looking at the record name \"14:11\" and the cron schedule running every 2 hours, it seems like these are daily check-in records being created at different times throughout the day, so the 14:11 timestamp likely refers to a check that ran at that time on some date. I'm noticing a critical timestamp discrepancy—the record shows a 2026-04-14 date when it should be 2026-04-17, which points to a data corruption or retrieval issue with the memory file rather than a timezone conversion problem. So the real issue is that the heartbeat state file gets written with the current time during each cron execution, but when I read those timestamps back, they're showing up as January dates instead of April—which means either the file is getting corrupted between writes or there's a mismatch in how the timestamps are being encoded and decoded.\n\nLooking at the sequence more carefully, there's a gap in the expected records: the heartbeat advances by two hours (from 14:11 to 16:11) but there's no corresponding check record created in between. This suggests the cron job might have fired for those intervals but the actual work—updating the heartbeat and creating the check record—didn't complete successfully, leaving orphaned heartbeat timestamps without the matching records that should accompany them. The most recent check record in the system is from 14:11 today, which means all the intermediate heartbeats through 16:11 are orphaned—there's no corresponding check record to tie them together. This explains why the 17:11 heartbeat shows as overdue when compared to the latest available record. The cron likely ran but failed silently, leaving the heartbeat timestamp at 1744992660000 without creating a new check record. Given that 54 records total (with 7 Dead status markers from previous runs) and the last successful check being 3+ hours ago, I should create a new check record now to mark the current state and report progress to the pool.\n\nLooking at the AI collaboration table, there's one task in progress and five pending, all assigned to 小妙. I'm also tracking the knowledge board with 54 total records: 3 marked Done, 5 historical Dead tasks, one blocked task for 小八, and one completed task for 小柱.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the remaining items—several Done tasks from 小柱, 小云, and the full team, plus multiple Dead